ted to the coffee  plant than it is anything else but it  does it it does have some analgesic  effects you know it does interact with  the opioid receptor so a lot of people  use it for pain management there's some  that take it and it's become really  effective for a lot of people getting  off very hard opiate substances and even  things like suboxone and methadone that  for a long time was the only way to get  off like a heroin addiction but then  they in they in turn created you into a  methadone or a a suboxone addict and  they even had far worse withdrawal  symptoms so this gave a lot of people a  chance at kind of a new life and so when  this you know when this this proposal to  schedule it came out it I mean it  crushed a lot of people it was it was  very scary to think that the government  would take away something that was  helping so many well it seems to me like  a total pharmaceutical industrial  complex attack right I mean when you  talk about something that all of a  sudden is going to schedule one  something that wasn't even on the radar  of so many people for so long at a time  when we're learning more and more about  its uses it seems like a money grab from  me and and the key thing is as you  mentioned it seems to be effective in  weaning people off of opiates and for  for many companies that means losing  money doesn't it and I think that's part  of it I think that's you know there's  also been a couple of trade groups from  the from the supplement industry that uh  that for a while now have tried to get  kratom x' legal clarification removed  and it completely taken off the market  maybe partly because they have to go  through such stringent laws that they  wanted everybody to but instead of  saying hey why isn't there better  regulation of this they wanted it banned  too and it comes and you come to find  out that some of these companies also  represent Bayer  BASF DuPont and these large chemical  companies  not just pharmaceutical but also  chemical companies that make quote  unquote supplements sure well listen  we're going to be following both what  goes on with kratom and what goes on at  Guantanamo Bay I really want to thank  you for sharing your story today we have  been speaking with Andrew Turner who's a  disabled veteran activist for veterans  rights and also a former Guantanamo Bay  prison guard I really appreciate you  talking to us today  David David can I share one last thing  please there's a there's a petition to  the White House going on right now if  somebody if anyone is interested in  signing it to keep kratom legal we're  still working on those petitions to the  White House this one you can find at the  American kratom associations website  just google American kratom Association  there's more information about the the  petition to the White House currently  we're just trying to keep it legal and  accessible for anyone that wants to have  that access

